    "body": "[comment]: # (Please do your best to search for duplicate issues before filing a new issue so we can keep our issue board clean)\n[comment]: # (Every issue should have exactly one feature request described in it. Please do not file feedback list tickets as it is difficult to parse them and address their individual points)\n[comment]: # (Feature Requests are better when they’re open-ended instead of demanding a specific solution e.g: “I want an easier way to do X” instead of “add Y”)\n[comment]: # (Read https://github.com/mozilla-mobile/fenix#i-want-to-file-an-issue for more information)\n\n### What is the user problem or growth opportunity you want to see solved?\ntracking and privacy.\n\n### How do you know that this problem exists today? Why is this important?\nhttps://sensor-js.xyz/\nand across various sites when the motion sensor permission is disabled in a chromium based browser on desktop.\n\n### Who will benefit from it?\ngeneral users. and no, not just people who care for their privacy (as all the privacy advocates say :P)\n\nI'd expect both a way to control this for all sites and to control it per-site, like chromium for desktop\n\nI say enable/disable because there needs to be a decision about defaults. It really should be an opt-in permission honestly but that'd require spec work.\n\nFunnily enough, Firefox for desktop doesn't provide websites with orientation, accelerometer and gyroscope data. Like Fenix, it doesn't provide users a setting to control this.\n\nThis is also related to another issue about per-site permission controls like Chromium for desktop.\n\nChromium for Android and desktop let you disable this permission in a user-facing setting.\n\njust for searchers, i'll put this here:\nsensor data from mobile devices using deviceorientation and devicemotion events\n\nedit:\nhmmm. https://bugs.chromium.org/p/chromium/issues/detail?id=947112\n\nhttps://www.w3.org/TR/orientation-event\n\n┆Issue is synchronized with this [Jira Task](https://mozilla-hub.atlassian.net/browse/FNXV2-8644)\n",
